\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD--AFRICA- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Kenyan shilling tied to Supreme Court ruling on repeat vote\b0\par\par\uc2 533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2017\par 14:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Nov 16 (Reuters) - Kenya's shilling will take its cue from the country's Supreme Court when it rules on the validity of a presidential election re-run held last month, while the Nigerian naira is likely to be supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 KENYA\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The shilling's direction in the next week will depend on the Supreme Court ruling on two petitions seeking to invalidate the re-election of President \uc2 Uhuru Kenyatta\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2 At 0734 GMT, commercial banks quoted the shilling at 103.60/80 per dollar compared with 103.45/65 at last Thursday's close.\par \par \uc2 The shilling weakened 0.5 percent when the court nullified the initial Aug. 8 presidential vote on Sept. 1 and ordered the re-run. The court has to deliver its verdict by Monday.\par \par \uc2 NIGERIA\par \par \uc2 The naira is expected to be stable in the week ahead, supported by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the injection of dollars into the market, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hovering at around 360 naira per dollar for investors, the same level as in the parallel market. On the official market, it has been quoted at around 305 per dollar for more than three months.\par \par \uc2 "It's been \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  all the way," one trader said.\par \par \uc2 GHANA\par \par \uc2 Ghana's cedi is expected to remain under pressure due to demand for dollars from importers and investors settling a domestic dollar bond issued last week, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The cedi, which had been fairly stable in the second half of this year, began slipping this month on a seasonal surge in demand for dollars. It was trading at 4.5550 to the dollar compared with 4.4400 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "The cedi could tumble again despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's presence on the market, especially amidst the seasonally high Q4 demand and speculative buying (of dollars)," said Joseph Biggles Amponsah, head of treasury at Northstar Home Finance.\par \par \uc2 ZAMBIA\par \par \uc2 The Zambian kwacha is likely to remain under pressure against the dollar next week due to sustained demand for hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from importers.\par \par \uc2 At 1154 GMT, commercial banks quoted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Africa's No.2 copper producer at 10.0000 per dollar from a close of 9.9000 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to continue to trade on the back foot as greenback demand appears to outweigh supply," \uc2 Zambia National Commercial Bank\uc2  (ZANACO) said in a market note.\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 The Tanzanian shilling is expected to appreciate slightly against the dollar over the coming week, buoyed by month-end dollar sales by firms to meet tax and salary obligations and export earnings from the agriculture sector.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 2,235/2,245 to the dollar on Thursday, stronger than 2,243/2,248 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "The shilling is expected to strengthen marginally against the dollar over the coming days due to the ongoing cashew nut export season," said a trader at Commercial Bank of Africa Tanzania. (Reporting by John Ndiso, Chijioke Ohuocha, Kwasi Kpodo, Chris Mfula and Fumbuka Ng'wanakilala; editing by John Stonestreet) \par \par \uc2 