\par\par \b \uc2 TEXT- \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  of Kenya interest rate decision\b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2015\par 16:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NAIROBI, Sept 22 (Reuters) - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kenya's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held its\par benchmark lending rate at 11.50 percent on Tuesday.\par     Below is the full Monetary Policy Committee statement on the\par decision:\par     \par MONETARY POLICY COMMITTEE MEETING \par The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) met on September 22, 2015,\par to review market developments and the outcomes of its previous\par monetary policy decisions. It noted that the monetary policy\par measures taken in the previous meetings were being transmitted\par into the economy. The MPC noted the following developments since\par its meeting in August 2015: \par  Overall month-on-month inflation decreased to 5.8 percent in\par August 2015, from 6.6 percent in July, moving closer to the 5\par percent target. Month-on-month non-food-non-fuel inflation\par reversed its upward trend since April, falling to 4.5 percent in\par August from 4.7 percent in July. The decrease in inflation was\par due to lower food prices and moderating demand pressures,\par partially offsetting the pass-through effects of exchange rate\par movements. \par  The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market was volatile in August and early\par September largely reflecting international developments, in\par particular the impact of the devaluation of the Chinese Yuan and\par continued strengthening of the U.S. dollar against most\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . However, the tight liquidity conditions and direct\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Kenya\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (CBK) helped\par stabilise the market. The current account deficit narrowed\par slightly in July 2015, due to a slowdown in imports and improved\par exports. In addition, diaspora remittances remain strong. \par  The CBK's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves stand at USD6,183.6\par million, which together with the Precautionary Arrangements with\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) continue to provide an\par adequate buffer against short-term shocks. Following the first\par review of these Arrangements in September, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  endorsed the\par country's macroeconomic policies and the measures adopted to\par address the spillover effects of the volatility in the global\par markets. \par  Consistent with the monetary policy stance, liquidity\par conditions remained tight with the average interbank interest\par rate above the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rate (CBR). Consequently, other\par short-term interest rates have been rising. \par  The banking sector is resilient although liquidity and credit\par risks remain. The CBK is closely monitoring the sector in view\par of the risks posed by volatility in the global markets. \par \par  Global economic growth remains moderate supported mainly by\par the U.S. economy. However, the slowdown in China has dampened\par the growth outlook for the global economy and increased\par volatility in the financial markets. Most emerging and frontier\par market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have depreciated against the U.S. dollar\par mainly due to the uncertainty around the timing of the increase\par in U.S. interest rates. \par \par The CBK's Market Perceptions Survey of September 2015 showed\par that private sector firms expect stronger growth in 2015\par compared to 2014, supported mainly by public investment in\par infrastructure and improved confidence in the economy. In\par addition, the Survey showed that inflation was expected to be\par stable in the remainder of 2015 supported by lower food and oil\par prices. However, the forecasted El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o rains could disrupt food\par supply chains and exert pressure on food prices in the short\par term. \par The Committee observed that the measures taken in the previous\par meetings had continued to bring inflation nearer to the 5\par percent target. However, the persistent turbulence in the global\par financial markets remain a risk to the inflation outlook, and\par its impact on the exchange rate should be monitored. The MPC\par concluded that the current monetary policy stance and supporting\par measures remain appropriate. The Committee therefore decided to\par retain the CBR at 11.50 percent in order to anchor inflation\par expectations. The CBK stands ready to use the instruments at its\par disposal to maintain overall price stability. \par DR. PATRICK NJOROGE \par CHAIRMAN, MONETARY POLICY COMMITTEE \par September 22, 2015 \par \par  (Editing by George Obulutsa)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-25T14:02:13.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150922eb9m00h1y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
