\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD-AFRICA- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -E.African  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  seen weaker, kwacha to firm\b0\par\par\uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2015\par 15:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Aug 27 (Reuters) - East African \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to come under pressure next week as investors flee emerging markets, while the naira is likely to hold its ground on anticipated dollar sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KENYA\par \par \uc2 Kenya's shilling is expected to stay under pressure next week with a flight by global investors from risky emerging market assets overshadowing domestic Kenyan issues.\par \par \uc2 At 1149 GMT, the shilling was quoted at 103.70/103.80 to the dollar, from 103.45/55 to the dollar a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "All markets are correcting and I don't think the shilling has been left behind," said one Nairobi-based trader. "It's all dependent on what's happening in the global markets next week."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Tuesday by selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 Traders say expectations of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action were providing some support against adverse global market conditions.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 The Ugandan shilling is expected to weaken, undermined by demand from importers for dollars.\par \par \uc2 At 1006 GMT commercial banks quoted it at 3,570/3,580, slightly stronger than last Thursday's close of 3,575/3,585.\par \par \uc2 "Going into next week I expect faster depreciation pressure because we'll likely see a rise in importer demand," a trader at a leading commercial bank said.\par \par \uc2 Importers will be buying hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added, to pay for raw material shipments to cover the next month.\par \par \uc2 The shilling is already down 22.6 percent so far this year, and could heading towards the 3,600 region.\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 The Tanzanian shilling is expected to slip against the dollar, undermined by demand from trading and oil companies.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 2,145/2,155 to the dollar on Thursday, weaker than 2,125/2,135 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "What we see is that the shilling will likely depreciate against the U.S. dollar next week due to demand from oil importers and trading companies," said Sameer Remtulla, a dealer at Commercial Bank of Africa Tanzania.\par \par \uc2 NIGERIA\par \par \uc2 The naira is seen sustaining its stability against the greenback next week on both the parallel and interbank markets in anticipation of dollar sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bureau de change operators and the interbank market.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the naira to remain unchanged at this level (210-211 a dollar) for as along as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to inject dollars into the market," said Harrison Owoh, a bureau de change operator.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold dollars to bureau de change operators twice a week since the start August in a bid to increase greenback liquidity and support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The naira was quoted at 211 to the dollar by many parallel operators in Lagos on Thursday, slightly weaker than 210 last week, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trading at 199.50 to the dollar on the interbank market, but was seen reaching the 197 peg set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February later in the day.\par \par \uc2 GHANA\par \par \uc2 Ghana's cedi is seen holding steady on interbank dollar sell-offs in anticipation of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  greenback sales beginning next week, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has signalled it will resume its vigorous interbank dollar sales in September to shore up the local unit, currently down 30 percent since January. GHS traded at 4.1100 at 1120 GMT on Thursday, compared to 4.1800 last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "We are beginning to see some stability due to (dollar) sell-off by investors and their banks in order to take advantage of the current attractive exchange rate before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes in," Joseph Biggles Amponsah, analyst at Dortis Research, said.\par \par \uc2 ZAMBIA\par \par \uc2 The kwacha is expected to recover some of its recent losses next week after China, a major importer of Zambia's copper, moved to support its economy by easing monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 At 1012 GMT on Thursday, commercial banks quoted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Africa's No. 2 copper producer at 8.4500 per dollar, weaker than 8.1700 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "Kwacha losses could be easing as it grinds out modest gains. Global markets are beginning to shed their losses," the local unit of South Africa's First National Bank (\uc2 FNB\uc2 ) said in a note on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The kwacha would also be boosted by dollar conversions to meet month-end obligations payable in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Zambia National Commercial Bank\uc2  said in a separate note. (Reporting by Drazen Jorgic, Fumbuka Ng'wanakilala, Elias Biryabarema, Oludare Mayowa, Kwasi Kpodo and Chris Mfula; Editing by Mark Heinrich) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-27T14:44:43.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150827eb8r00epf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
