\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Kenya \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  lifts shilling, shares fall\b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2015\par 16:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Shilling could weaken after Islamist attack\par     * Shares fall after attack hurt sentiment\par \par  (Updates with market close, shares)\par     NAIROBI, April 7 (Reuters) - Kenya's shilling \par strengthened on Tuesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold dollars to\par banks, while stocks fell on negative sentiment after a deadly\par attack by Islamists in a town near the frontier with Somalia.\par     At the close of trade, commercial banks quoted the shilling\par at 92.55/65 to the dollar, stronger than an intraday low of\par 92.82/92. The shilling had closed at 92.70/80 on Thursday.\par     The markets were closed for holidays on Friday and Monday.\par     "They came in at about 1 pm," said a trader at a commercial\par bank, referring to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which had sold an\par unspecified amount of the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Traders had said the shilling may weaken following the\par attack that was likely to affect tourism, a key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par earner for East Africa's biggest economy. \par     Hoteliers said tourists cancelled their bookings after the\par attack by the Somali militant group \f28 \uc2 \f2 al Shabaab\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par     Kenya's tourism industry has been in decline since 2013 when\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 al Shabaab\f28 \uc2 \f2  stormed an upscale shopping mall in the capital\par Nairobi, killing 67 people during a bloody four-day siege.\par     Traders forecast the shilling, which has lost 2.3 percent so\par far this year, would trade in a tight range of 92.50-93.00 per\par dollar in the next few days as the fallout from the attack is\par balanced out by more likely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     On the stock market, the benchmark NSE-20 share index\par  lost 0.33 percent to close at 5,179.76 points, largely\par on negative sentiment following last week's attack.\par     On the debt market, bonds worth 1.8 billion shillings ($19\par million) were traded, up from 1.0 billion shillings worth of\par bonds traded on Thursday.\par     \par                ...........................Shilling spot rates\par                   .....................Shilling forward rates\par                            .......................Cross rates\par          ..................................Local contributors\par            \f28 \uc2 \f2 .......................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Kenya\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index\par           .....................Kenyan Bonds contributor pages\par                           ...............Treasury bill yields\par         ..................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par         .........................Horizontal repo transactions\par          ,       ................Daily interbank lending rate\par               .............................Kenya Bond pricing\par              ..................Real time Africa economic data\par  <ECI & AFR> ...........................African economic news\par           .................................NSE-20 Share Index\par          .................................NSE All Share Index\par              ...........................FT NSE Kenya 15 Index\par 