\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD-AFRICA- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Presidential election outcome to buoy Nigerian naira\b0\par\par\uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2015\par 18:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LAGOS, April 2 (Reuters) - Nigeria's naira could firm against the dollar on the parallel market next week after a peaceful election but Kenya's shilling could weaken after a deadly attack by Islamists which could further hit tourism.\par \par \uc2 NIGERIA\par \par \uc2 The naira firmed to 210 to the dollar on the parallel market from 226 a week ago after opposition leader Muhammadu Buhari won in a vote that avoided the violence of previous polls.\par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded within the 199-199.50 band on the official interbank market, where it has been stuck since February, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged the rate.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of relief, individuals who had stockpiled dollars to hedge against political risk fearing the election could be marred by violence were exchanging their funds for the naira.\par \par \uc2 "We are not going to see much of movement in the pricing of the naira at the interbank market until the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reviews the present measure which has stagnated the rate," a dealer said.\par \par \uc2 KENYA\par \par \uc2 The Kenyan shilling is expected to weaken, hurt by negative sentiment after a deadly attack by al Shaabab near the frontier with Somalia threatened its tourism fortunes.\par \par \uc2 The shilling has lost ground steadily since last year, partly due to a downturn in tourism following attacks by \uc2 al Shabaab\uc2  militants. Tourists are a leading source of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for East Africa's biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 The shilling could trade between 92.50-93.20 to the dollar, with a move beyond 93 likely triggering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Chris Muiga said.\par \par \uc2 Traders say a government bond could lend support to the shilling. \uc2 Kenya's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has invited bids for a 12-year infrastructure bond worth 25 billion shillings ($270 million).\par \par \uc2 GHANA\par \par \uc2 Ghana's cedi could be buoyed by demand from businesses looking to settle domestic quarterly bills.\par \par \uc2 The West African \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened by about 11 percent since January, but looks set to reverse some of those losses, partly helped by a $940 million \uc2 IMF\uc2  aid deal this month that is expected to unlock additional offshore inflows.\par \par \uc2 "Liquidity has been weak on the local market ... and this is likely to hold the dollar/cedi at current levels next week," \uc2 Barclays Bank\uc2  analyst Michael Akpakli said.\par \par \uc2 He said demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would rise ahead of public holidays on Friday and Monday.\par \par \uc2 ZAMBIA\par \par \uc2 The outlook points to a firmer Zambian kwacha next week, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked the amount of money commercial banks should deposit with the regulator and as the government plans to resolve a mining tax row with foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Zambia\uc2  will raise the statutory reserve ratio to 18 percent from 14 percent next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The increase will reduce liquidity and that should render support to the kwacha," analyst Maambo Hamaundu said.\par \par \uc2 President Edgar Lungu last month directed the finance and mining ministers to adjust royalties on mining firms by April 8, saying the copper-producer could consider temporarily reverting to the less punitive tax regime which was in place in 2014.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 Traders predicted a stable shilling on tightening liquidity but some investors were concerned about the impact of a planned increase in government spending.\par \par \uc2 The Ugandan government plans to increase spending before the 2016 elections, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Uganda\uc2  has vowed to use its key interest rate to keep inflation in check.\par \par \uc2 The shilling traded at 3,000/3,016, weaker than 2,975/2,985 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "Liquidity is getting tighter in the market, we see this tightening slowing any weakening of the unit (shilling)," said Ahmed Kalule, trader at Bank of Africa.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 92.6500 Kenyan shillings) (Reporting by Oludare Mayowa, Elias Biryabarema, Kwasi Kpodo and Chris Mfula; Compiled by Stella Mapenzauswa; Editing by James Macharia) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-4-2T17:13:30.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150402eb4200hzl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
