\par\par \b \uc2 Kenyan shilling, stocks shrug off GDP rise to hold steady\b0\par\par\uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2014\par 16:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Shilling steadied by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mop-up\par     * Stocks barely changed\par \par     By George Obulutsa\par     NAIROBI, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The Kenyan shilling and\par main stock market index were flat on Tuesday, shrugging off a\par jump in the gross domestic product of East Africa's biggest\par economy after the authorities changed the base calculation year.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stock market traders said both markets\par had factored in a rise of Kenya's GDP, now estimated to be 25\par percent bigger after the base calculation year was changed to \par 2009 from 2001. \par     At close of trade at 1300 GMT, commercial banks quoted the\par shilling at 89.30/40 to the dollar, the same as Monday's close.\par     Traders said the shilling oscillated either side of that\par level to touch an intraday high of 89.00/20 and a low of\par 89.45/55 to the dollar.\par     The moves were driven by importer demand for dollars, which\par weakened the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but this was countered when the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mopped up excess liquidity.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often soaks up excess shilling liquidity\par through repos or other instruments, a move that tends to support\par the shilling by making it more expensive to hold dollars. \par     The shilling has broadly weakened in recent weeks, and has\par closed in on 89.50 where the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold dollars to offer\par support last week. But traders say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was unlikely\par to step in for now as long as weakness appeared to reflect a\par need for dollars rather than speculation.\par     During the session, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mopped up 8.75 billion \par shillings ($98.31 million) in excess liquidity. It had sought to\par absorb 9 billion shillings.\par     "We saw the CBK in to mop up some shillings in the repo.\par That gave it some momentary  support," Nahashon Mungai, trader\par at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Kenya\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Commercial Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\par     "Regulatory \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is what's been watched at the\par moment, and all this dollar demand. I suppose that's why the\par shilling is on the back foot."\par     Traders said they forecast the shilling, which has lost 3\par percent to the dollar so far this year, to trade in the 89.00 to\par 89.60 range in coming days.\par     On the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nairobi Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  Exchange, the main NSE-20 Share\par Index was barely changed, easing by 2.19 points or 0.04\par percent, to close at 5,255.62 points.\par     On the secondary market, government bonds valued at 4.34\par billion shillings were traded, up from 1.24 billion shillings\par traded on Monday.\par     \par                ...........................Shilling spot rates\par                   .....................Shilling forward rates\par                            .......................Cross rates\par          ..................................Local contributors\par            \f28 \uc2 \f2 .......................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Kenya\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index\par           .....................Kenyan Bonds contributor pages\par                           \f28 \uc2 \f2 .