\par\par \b \uc2 Kenyan shilling steady, seen weaker on pent-up dollar demand\b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2014\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NAIROBI, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The Kenyan shilling was\par flat on Tuesday but was expected to weaken due to expected\par demand from importers for dollars amid scant hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par inflows.\par     At 0830 GMT, commercial banks quoted the shilling unchanged\par at 88.90/89.00 to the dollar.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of East Africa's biggest economy on Monday\par slipped briefly to a 32-month low of 88.95/89.05, Reuters data\par showed. \par     Traders said there was little inflow of dollars from the tea\par and tourism sectors, which are leading \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earners,\par to meet increased demand from importers.\par     The market was also awash with the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due to\par renewed government spending and bond maturities, traders said. \par     "The shilling is still on the back foot. The only reprieve\par would be the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by selling dollars,"\par Nahashon Mungai, trader at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Kenya\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Commercial Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said.\par     "Fundamentals show more demand than supply of dollars."    \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has regularly soaked up excess liquidity in\par the money markets since last year. \par     On Monday the bank drained excess liquidity for the eighth\par trading session in a row. The action supports the shilling by\par making it more costly to hold dollars. \par     However, some traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have to sell\par dollars to defend the shilling from weakening further. \par     Some other traders said this action would only serve to\par deplete the bank's own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, arguing that\par it was difficult to successfully reverse the tide against market\par fundamentals, where dollar demand far outstrips inflows.\par     In late August, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold dollars into the\par market after the shilling hit 88.80/90, which at the time was \par its lowest level since December 2011. \par     Traders said they expected the shilling, which has lost\par about 2.6 percent against the dollar this year, would  trade in\par the 88.60 to 89.10 range this week.\par     \par  ...........................Shilling spot rates \par  .....................Shilling forward rates \par   .......................Cross rates \par ..................................Local contributors \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 .......................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Kenya\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index \par .....................Kenyan Bonds contributor pages \par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 ...............Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  bill yields \par ..................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par .........................Horizontal repo transactions \par ,................Daily interbank lending rate \par .............................Kenya Bond pricing \par ..................Real time Africa economic data \par <ECI & AFR> ...........................African economic news\par .................................NSE-20 Share Index\par ............................