\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Kenyan shilling weaker on bomb blasts, shares up\b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2014\par 17:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates with share moves, market reaction to bombings)\par     By Richard Lough and Drazen Jorgic\par     NAIROBI, May 16 (Reuters) - The Kenyan shilling lost\par ground against the dollar on Friday, hurt by back-to-back\par bombings in the capital Nairobi which knocked market confidence\par in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while shares rose.\par     At the 1300 GMT market close, the shilling was at 87.50/60\par to the dollar, weaker than the previous session's 87.34/45\par close.\par     Andlip Nazir, senior trader at \f28 \uc2 \f2 I&M Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par has come under pressure from a spate of negative news, including\par the latest blasts in which at least 10 people were killed and\par close to 70 wounded. \par     Tourist arrivals, a key source of hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , have been\par cut by frequent gun and grenade attacks after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Kenya intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par against Islamist militants in neighbouring Somalia. Britain, the\par United States and other Western governments have warned\par holidaymakers against visiting Kenya. \par     "These travel advisories are being issued left, right and\par centre. Nothing positive is coming out of the market," Nazir\par said.\par     Frequent liquidity mop-ups by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Kenya\f28 \uc2 \f2  have\par limited the shilling's falls but traders say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par likely to come under pressure again next week.\par     "CBK has been trying to mop up but that's had little effect\par on the shilling," Nazir added. "The direction remains for a\par weaker shilling."\par     The benchmark NSE-20 Share Index gained 0.4 percent\par to close the session at 4,967.57 points, lifted higher by\par Kenya's leading telecoms firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Safaricom\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Safaricom\f28 \uc2 \f2  hit an intra-day high of 13.20 shillings before\par easing off to end the day at 12.85 shillings, up 2 percent on\par Thursday's closing price. The stock accounted for 57.8 million\par of the 70.8 million shares traded.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Safaricom\f28 \uc2 \f2  reported a 23.8 percent jump in its annual core\par profits earlier this week, raising its dividend by 52 percent to\par 0.47 shillings per share.    \par                ...........................Shilling spot rates \par                   .....................Shilling forward rates \par                            .......................Cross rates \par          ..................................Local contributors \par            \f28 \uc2 \f2 .......................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Kenya\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index \par           .....................Kenyan Bonds contributor pages \par                           ...............Treasury bill yields \par         ..................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par         .........................Horizontal repo transactions \par          ,       ................Daily interbank lending rate \par               .............................Kenya Bond pricing \par              ..................Real time Africa economic data \par  <ECI & AFR> ...........................African economic news\par           .................................NSE-20 Share Index\par          .................................NSE All Share Index\par