\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Kenya shilling extends losses on importer dollar demand\b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2014\par 17:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds closing price, stocks)\par     NAIROBI, May 8 (Reuters) - Importer demand for dollars\par weakened Kenya's shilling on Thursday, but expectations the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would sell hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the shilling falls much\par further was seen stemming further losses.\par     Commercial banks priced the shilling at 87.10/20 at the 1300\par GMT market close, 0.1 percent down on Wednesday.\par     "We expect an 87.00-87.40 range in the short term," one\par trader in the market said, adding that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Kenya\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par (CBK) had previously \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  around 87.30 or 87.50. "We\par should expect the CBK to act, going beyond the mop-ups and\par selling dollars in the market."\par     The regulator has frequently siphoned off excess liquidity\par this year to help shore up the shilling, which traders say has\par been put under sustained pressure by weak dollar inflows from\par the tea, coffee and tourism industries.\par     Tourism numbers have been hurt by a series of gun and bomb\par attacks blamed on Islamist militants, including the September\par assault on an upscale Nairobi shopping mall that killed 67\par people. Weekend bomb attacks in Nairobi and Mombasa rattled\par market confidence again.\par     On the benchmark NSE 20 Share Index, Equity bank\par  led shares higher as foreign investors bet on a\par continuation of strong first quarter earnings.\par     Equity, Kenya's largest lender by depositors, extended a\par month-long rally to hit and intra-day high of 42 shillings per\par share, a record high since the bank carried out a share split in\par 2009. It closed up 3.8 percent at 41.25 shillings. \par     Ronald Lugalia, an analyst at Nairobi-based AIB Capital,\par said speculation rather than a shift in the bank's fundamentals\par was driving the rally. "Foreign investors are buying," Lugalia\par said.\par     Equity posted a 21 percent jump in first-quarter pretax\par profit in its first quarter, although profit growth was curbed\par by rising bad debts and conflict in neighbouring South Sudan\par where the bank has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     \par                ...........................Shilling spot rates \par                   .....................Shilling forward rates \par                            .......................Cross rates \par          ..................................Local contributors \par            \f28 \uc2 \f2 .......................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Kenya\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index \par           .....................Kenyan Bonds contributor pages \par                           ...............Treasury bill yields \par         ..................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par         .........................Horizontal repo transactions \par          ,       ................Daily interbank lending rate \par               .............................Kenya Bond pricing \par              ..................Real time Africa economic data \par  <ECI & AFR> ...........................African economic news\par           .................................NSE-20 Share Index\par          .................................NSE All Share Index\par              ...........................FT NSE Kenya 15 Index\par              ................