\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Kenyan shares ease, shilling slightly weaker\b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2014\par 16:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Main share index down, Mumias sugar drags it lower\par     * Shilling eases slightly, seen supported by mop-up\par \par  (Adds markets close, stocks, bonds)\par     By George Obulutsa\par     NAIROBI, April 2 (Reuters) - Kenya's main share index fell\par 0.5 percent on Wednesday, dragged lower by shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mumias\par Sugar\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which said it had suspended its chief executive\par officer and commercial director. The shilling weakened.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mumias Sugar\f28 \uc2 \f2  closed down 2.9 percent at 3.30 Kenyan\par shillings ($0.04). It had recovered from a low of 5.9 percent\par earlier in the session, a level it last saw on Feb. 25.\par     The company's chairman said Mumias's board had asked Chief\par Executive Peter Kebati and Commercial Director \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paul Murgor\f28 \uc2 \f2  to\par "step aside" while it investigates the firm's sales and\par distribution deals. \par     "The CEO is a key figure in the business, and if he is\par suspended for whatever reason, then definitely that has an\par impact. It sends the wrong signal for the stock," said Augustine\par Misoka, trader at Sterling Investment Bank.\par     The Nairobi Securities Exchange's main NSE-20 Share Index\par closed down 26.40 points at 4,933.56 points.\par     At the 1300 GMT close of trade in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market,\par commercial banks quoted the shilling at 86.50/60 to the\par dollar, compared with Tuesday's close of 86.40/50.\par     Traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eased slightly because of demand\par for dollars from the energy sector late in the session.\par     Traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's mopping up of excess\par shilling liquidity from the money markets was expected to offer\par support to the shilling in the coming days.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Kenya\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been regularly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the money markets to drain excess shilling liquidity in recent\par weeks, a trend which traders say should boost the local\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "With CBK mopping up aggressively, the expected trend is for\par a strengthening Kenya shilling," Sheikh Mehran, senior trader at\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kenya Commercial Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday mopped up 4.1 billion\par shillings from the money markets using repurchase agreements.\par     Traders said they forecast the shilling to trade in the\par 86.20 to 86.80 range in coming days.\par     On the secondary market, government bonds valued at 2.16\par billion shillings were traded, down from 2.63 billion shillings\par on Tuesday.\par     \par                ...........................Shilling spot rates\par                   .....................Shilling forward rates\par                            .......................Cross rates\par          ..................................Local contributors\par            \f28 \uc2 \f2 .......................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Kenya\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index\par           .....................Kenyan Bonds contributor pages\par                           ...............Treasury bill yields\par         ..................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f