\par\par \b \uc2 Kenya's hunger for imports risks more  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  forays\b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2013\par 16:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Reuters poll sees shilling falling to 88 per dollar by year-end\par \par \uc2 * Kenya's current account deficit weighs on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Weaker shilling could signal end to rate cuts cycle\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Mwanza\par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, May 30 (Reuters) - Renewed economic activity following a smooth presidential vote is driving demand for imports in Kenya, weakening the shilling and prompting wariness about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's response, including more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in four months on Wednesday and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in east Africa's biggest economy said in the aftermath it is likely to do so again if the shilling starts to decline.\par \par \uc2 The shilling had fallen 1.7 percent in just over a week to an eight-week low of 85.25/45 per dollar before Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , tumbling on strong demand for hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from importers,\par \par \uc2 It was at 85.00/85.20 on Thursday, slightly weaker than its 84.85/84.95 close the previous day. Traders viewed this as stable but said the bank would come in again if the weakening trend picked up.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on top of the game and might keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever the shilling comes under pressure," said Ignatius Chicha, head of markets at Citibank.\par \par \uc2 "We are going to have more demand than supply as we see resumption in economic activity."\par \par \uc2 Demand for imported goods has picked up along with the economy since it became apparent that the March election was not going to lead to the kind of violence the followed the previous one.\par \par \uc2 But this could weigh on the shilling and could feed into inflation if left unchecked.\par \par \uc2 In late 2011, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to hike interest rates sharply to rein-in imported inflation that had pushed the shilling to a record low of 107 per dollar in October that year.\par \par \uc2 At the time, the current account deficit - the difference between a country's imports and exports - had risen to above 10 percent. The current account deficit stands at above 12 percent of GDP, driven by increased local demand for foreign goods.\par \par \uc2 Peter Mutuku, head of trading at Bank of Africa, said the shillings depreciation was inevitable "because of the current account mismatch".\par \par \uc2 The shilling is still 1.6 percent stronger to the dollar so far in 2013, but is widely expected to weaken by year-end, pressured by Kenya's persistently high current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of eight analysts and traders gave a median forecast of 88.00 shillings per dollar by year end.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained active in the market, regularly sterilising liquidity through actively soaking up liquidity since last year to support the shilling by making it slightly expensive to hold long dollar positions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been selling hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as it cut its borrowing costs by 950 basis points since July 2012 to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 Some traders said a weaker shilling could tempt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse course on a cycle of interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 "We could see interest rates go back up but that is a last resort because they (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) are keen on growth at the moment," said a trader at one commercial bank.\par \par \uc2 However, for now, they said the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of at least 0.27 months cover above the statutory four months worth of import cover, had given it room to sell even more dollars in the market.\par \par \uc2 "Looks like the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has managed to cool off excess demand for now and is keenly watching the market," said Dickson Magecha, a trader at Standard chartered Bank. (Editing by James Macharia/Jeremy Gaunt)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 KENYA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 / | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | AFN | G | RBN | ABX | BNX | FUN | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130530e95u000qd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
