\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Kenya \highlight\uc2  shilling softens vs dollar, traders eye cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2013\par 10:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Jan 24 (Reuters) - The Kenyan shilling weakened against the dollar on Thursday as importers and manufacturers sold ahead of the presidential election in March, and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in to the market to support the shilling.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks posted the shilling at 87.50/70 per dollar at 0733 GMT, weaker than Wednesday's close of 87.35/55. The shilling is down 1.6 percent year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 Traders said lower interest rates in the market are making it less attractive to hold shillings, while growing demand for greenbacks was fuelled by uncertainty over the first presidential election since the 2007 poll, when ethnic violence brought the country's economy to its knees.\par \par \uc2 "There has been (dollar) demand this morning from manufacturers, and we have also seen petroleum guys," said Peter Mutuku, a trader at \uc2 Bank of Africa\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We are heading towards end-month and people feel the longer they wait, the higher the (dollar) is likely to be."\par \par \uc2 But traders added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the session if the shilling touched support at 88.00.\par \par \uc2 "We're seeing a lot of (dollar) demand without matching inflows. 88.00 is the support right now, but could be taken out quite easily," said Duncan Kinuthia, head of trading at \uc2 Commercial Bank of Africa\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dollar demand on Wednesday drove the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a one-year low of 87.75/95 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Kenya's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reacted to the weakening shilling by selling an unspecified amount of dollars directly to commercial banks for the fifth time this year.\par \par \uc2 The bank has also been mopping up excess shillings in the market via repurchase agreements to stem the sharp depreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Thursday it sought to absorb 6 billion shillings.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             ...........................Shilling spot rates\par                .....................Shilling forward rates\par                         .......................Cross rates\par       ..................................Local contributors\par         \f28 \uc2 \f2 .......................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Kenya\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index\par        .....................Kenyan Bonds contributor pages\par                        ...............Treasury bill yields\par      ..................\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par      .........................Horizontal repo transactions\par       ,       ................Daily interbank lending rate\par            .............................Kenya Bond pricing\par           ..................Real time Africa economic data\par <ECI & AFR> ...........................African economic news\par        .................................NSE-20 Share Index\par       .................................NSE All Share Index\par           ...........................FT NSE Kenya 15 Index\par           .......................... FT NSE Kenya 25 Index\par  SPEED GUIDES:\par                                  \par          \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Beatrice Gachenge and Kevin Mwanza; Editing by Drazen Jorgic and Hugh Lawson)\f28 \par \par \uc2 KENYA-MARKETS/ | ABN | D | M | RBN | AFN 