\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-AFRICA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ghana cedi flirts with record low\b0\par\par\uc2 1,376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2011\par 16:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Nigeria)\par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Sept 15 (Reuters) - Ghana's cedi is in danger of sliding to a record low against the dollar next week, while Kenya's shilling, which has been hammered throughout the year, may finally find support from a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate increase.\par \par \uc2 GHANA\par \par \uc2 The cedi may well plumb an all-time low against the dollar after a sudden bout of weakness in the last two days triggered by hefty corporate demand for the greenback and limited supply, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Christopher Nettey of Standard Bank quoted the cedi at 1.5690/15 on Thursday, softer than yesterday's close of 1.5665/90. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plumbed a record low of 1.575 on Feb. 3, according to Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 "I expect the cedi to drop down to as low as 1.58 due to pressure from offshore investors buying dollars from our market," Nettey said.\par \par \uc2 Other traders also expected further falls in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has surprised analysts with its sudden decline.\par \par \uc2 The cedi normally weakens towards the end of the year as importers step up dollar purchases to pay for Christmas-related goods. September is considered too early for this to be having an effect.\par \par \uc2 "The cedi's depreciation has come as a surprise especially when we are not yet in the last quarter of the year," said Biggles Amponsah of Access Bank.\par \par \uc2 "I have seen dollar inflows from NGOs and mining support services this week but this was not enough to stop the U.S. dollar from further gains."\par \par \uc2 Traders have also attributed the softness to a lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 KENYA\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 's shilling , which has lost 17.5 percent against the dollar this year, may gain as the impact of a 75 basis point \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate increase slowly sinks in, taking the edge off panic dollar buying, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Despite initial disappointment at the extent of the tightening, traders said the decision made at an extraordinary Monetary Policy Committee on Wednesday should be broadly supportive of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 1013 GMT, the shilling was posted at 94.75/95 against the dollar, weaker than last week's close of 93.40/60, when President Kibaki said the government had requested an early disbursement of an IMF credit facility.\par \par \uc2 "I see us trading in a wide range with a bias for the shilling strengthening. The rate hike should be shilling positive," said Standard Chartered trader Dickson Magecha.\par \par \uc2 "On reflection it can be said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took an aggressive stance to try and contain inflation. The panic buying (of dollars) that was there is now subsiding."\par \par \uc2 Traders said they expected the shilling to trade within 93.50-95.50 range next week, with a bias for strengthening.\par \par \uc2 The shilling recovered from a new all-time low of 95.65 against the dollar on Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars for the second time in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 Uganda's shilling , which has shed nearly 10 percent against the dollar so far this year, may weaken slightly due to demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the energy sector and other importers.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 2,780/2,790, from last Thursday's close of 2,790/2,800.\par \par \uc2 "We're moving toward the last quarter of the year and around this time dollar demand accelerates sharply as businesses start to import for year-end shopping and festivities," said Rogers Lutaaya, a trader at Bank of Africa.\par \par \uc2 "The oil sector is also buying a lot of dollars and the supplies from offshore investors are still way too insufficient to match."\par \par \uc2 Faisal Bukenya, head of market-making at Barclays Bank, said a heavily oversubscribed 80 billion shilling ($28.7 million) government bond auction this week showed strong foreign interest in government paper, although the sums were too small to make a dent in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The size of the auction was very small and so dollar inflows from offshore investors in government paper are a drop in the ocean compared to the level of dollar demand."\par \par \uc2 Yields on both bonds shot up on the back of Bank of tighter monetary policy designed to rein in inflation, presently at 21.4 percent, and support the weak shilling.\par \par \uc2 Traders said 2,800 was likely to be the shilling's resistance level, and 2,870 support.\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 Tanzania's shilling , which hit a record low of 1,640 against the dollar this week, is likely to stay on the back foot due to oil sector dollar demand although some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows could offer support.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 1,636/1,641 to the dollar, compared with last Thursday's close of 1,626/1,631.\par \par \uc2 "We are seeing a gradual depreciation of the shilling. There is some demand from oil companies on a one-off basis, but nothing pressing," said Eric Chijoriga, a dealer at National Bank of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 "Some inflows from the tourism sector have helped to ease pressure. We expect to see more inflows from corporates after mid-month when they off-load dollars for salaries and tax commitments."\par \par \uc2 Traders said they expected the shilling to trade in the 1,630s in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 During the week to Wednesday, the \uc2 Bank of Tanzania\uc2  said it traded $81.57 million on the Interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 10.6 percent against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 ZAMBIA\par \par \uc2 Zambia's kwacha is expected to post gains after several \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last two weeks, although this could unwind if Sept. 20 elections are marred by violence or end up with a disputed result, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Africa's top copper producer climbed 1.4 percent on Thursday to 4,870 against the dollar from 4,940 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 Although the elections have caused some portfolio outflows, Wiggins Mupango, Standard Chartered's local head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, predicted kwacha gains before and after polling day, which is expected to pass off peacefully.\par \par \uc2 "Only a break above the 5,000 level will negate our bullish view. The downtrend is envisaged to find support around the 4,850 level," Mupango said.\par \par \uc2 "We will occasionally see some spikes as the market reacts to broad dollar strength against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but we do not anticipate any trickle-down effect on the kwacha."\par \par \uc2 President Rupiah Banda has a narrow lead over opposition leader Michael Sata although a number of undecided voters mean an upset is still possible, according to an opinion poll published last week.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has traded in the 4,600-4,800 band for most of the last 12 months, although the looming elections have caused it to weaken outside that band in the last month.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown a lot of reluctance for the kwacha to go beyond 5,000," said Nema Ramkhelawan, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Rand Merchant Bank\uc2  in Johannesburg.\par \par \uc2 "That's been a key resistance level and we've seen them \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on quite a few occasions in the last two weeks."\par \par \uc2 NIGERIA\par \par \uc2 The naira is seen weakening further against the dollar unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases the volume of dollar sales at its bi-weekly auction to douse mounting pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The naira was trading at 156.37 to the dollar on the interbank market on Thursday, weaker than Wednesday's close of 156.20.\par \par \uc2 Traders said strong demand for dollars from gasoline importers and contractors handling power projects was forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow the naira to depreciate at the bi-weekly auction.\par \par \uc2 Demand at the bi-weekly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction surged to $510 million on Wednesday, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold only $350 million at 153.54 to the dollar. The regulator sold $350 million at 153.27 per dollar at Monday's auction.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the bulk of unmet demand at the official window was channeled to the interbank market, putting further pressure on the naira.\par \par \uc2 "The naira will remain under pressure unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to increase the volume of dollars on sales at the official window next week," one dealer said\par \par \uc2 AFRICA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110915e79f000zp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
