\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Kenya tourism arrivals up 16 pct in Jan-April\b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2011\par 11:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Kenya shilling weakness could boost sector - minister\par \par \uc2 * Tourism up after 2008 violence, global financial crisis\par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, June 28 (Reuters) - The number of tourists visiting Kenya in the first four months of 2011 rose by 16 percent from a year earlier, and a weaker local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could boost the number of visitors in the short term, the tourism minister said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Tourist arrivals in east Africa's biggest economy rose to 390,148 from 336,179 in the same period a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Tourism generated 74 billion shillings ($817.6 million) in 2010 for east Africa's biggest economy, making it one of the country's leading sources of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The sector has recovered after it was hit in 2008 by post-election violence and the lingering effects of the global financial crisis, which hurt arrivals in 2009.\par \par \uc2 "These figures shows positive growth," Tourism Minister Najib Balala told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Balala said the weakening of the shilling could benefit the sector, but warned that the wider implications of a weaker shilling on the economy could increase the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 "When the shilling is too weak ... it can be good for one sector but not necessarily for the entire economy and the people of Kenya, so we need to find a balance," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Definitely there will be an increase of interest (from tourists) but I don't believe that this is the main reason (for the growth in tourist arrivals). The weakening of the shilling is temporary," he said.\par \par \uc2 Balala said he backed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the shilling against the dollar, but noted that prices of food imports were likely to rise.\par \par \uc2 The shilling hit a new low against the dollar of 91.90 last Wednesday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has resisted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the shilling's slide.\par \par \uc2 The bank has said \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2  had adequate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves but would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, not to influence the level or direction of the shilling.\par \par \uc2 ($1=90.50 Kenyan Shilling)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Humphrey Malalo; Writing by James Macharia; Editing by Susan Fenton)\par \par \uc2 (Email:nairobi.newsroom@reuters.com; Tel +254 20 2224 717))\par \par \uc2 (For more Reuters Africa coverage and to have your say on the top issues, visit: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://af.reuters.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://af.reuters.com/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 KENYA-TOURISM/ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110628e76s000eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
