\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-AFRICA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  WEEKAHEAD-Kenya shilling to continue slide\b0\par\par\uc2 1,147 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2011\par 17:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Ghana cedi outlook)\par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, May 19 (Reuters) - Kenya's shilling could hit new lows against the dollar next week while its Ugandan peer will probably have to rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stay on an even keel.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 KENYA\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 's shilling <KES=>, which hit all-time lows on Wednesday and Thursday, is expected to keep weakening if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and importers step up dollar buying as usual towards the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Kenya\uc2  (CBK) has bought 28 million euros and mopped up 6 billion shillings, which it said on Thursday was meant to build reserves and cover for external payments amounting to $9.1 million. [ID:nLDE74I0F1]\par \par \uc2 At 1226 GMT, commercial banks quoted the shilling at 86.20/30 against the dollar, after it earlier hit an all-time low of 87.15 sparked by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of euros.\par \par \uc2 "People are profit-taking on long dollar positions. CBK's mop-up and statement today helped shift sentiment on the shilling," said Julius Kiriinya, a trader at African Banking Corporation.\par \par \uc2 "If they (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) come back in and banks start building up on their positions next week, the shilling could weaken towards 87.00," he added.\par \par \uc2 Traders expect the shilling to trade in the 86.00-87.00 range against the dollar, with a bias towards weakening.\par \par \uc2 "The shilling could weaken further as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (buying of) euros coincides with oil importers' purchases and end-year dividends being paid," said Dickson Magecha, a senior trader at Standard Chartered Bank.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 Uganda's shilling <UGX=> is likely to stay weak amid fears of further violent political protests, although it probably will not breach the psychological 2,400 level.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2,389/2,393 to the dollar, a shade weaker than 2,384/2,389 a week ago, when the inauguration of President Yoweri Museveni was marred by deadly clashes between police and opposition supporters.\par \par \uc2 Spiralling fuel and food prices are adding to the volatile political atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think it will breach 2,400," Phillip Ssali, a trader at Standard Chartered Bank Uganda, said. "The demand (for dollars) is there but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has expressed interest to sell at about 2,400 so that's the factor to watch."\par \par \uc2 Traders said they saw the shilling range-bound between 2,385 and 2,395. It hit a record low of 2,424/2,429 on March 15.\par \par \uc2 The shilling has hovered near 2,400 for two weeks and traders blame the unrest, though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not. The bank sold dollars to bolster the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on two days last week but it made little impact.\par \par \uc2 "Demand and supply have evened out," Faisal Bukenya of Barclays Bank Uganda said. "It will stay at the same levels that it did this week" -- though analysis of the 14-day and 50-day simple moving averages suggests the shilling will stay on a weakening trend in the near term.\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 Tanzania's shilling <TZS=> weakened on Thursday due to strong dollar demand from the oil sector and traders expect it to remain under pressure until mid-June when the agriculture export season kicks in.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted it at 1,530/1,538 to the dollar compared with 1,515/1,520 at last Thursday's close.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling dollars in the market not to support the shilling, but for liquidity purposes," said African Banking Corporation trader Gladness Deogratias.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the shilling will reach the 1,550 level. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should start to gain ground within two to three weeks when we reach the agricultural export season."\par \par \uc2 Traders said the shilling was likely to trade in the 1,520-1,530 range.\par \par \uc2 "Oil companies are importing fuel in bulk quantities against the backdrop of rising oil prices at the world market. This has triggered demand," said Msafiri Absolom, a trader at Tanzania Investment Bank.\par \par \uc2 "We expect inflows from crop exports in mid-June to stabilise the market, so the shilling will most likely appreciate in the second half of the year."\par \par \uc2 In the last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it traded $38.7 million on its interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 NIGERIA\par \par \uc2 The naira <NGN=D1> is expected to fall amid strong dollar demand and a lack of local supply.\par \par \uc2 It was trading at 156.92 to the dollar on Thursday from its Wednesday close of 156.70 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to meet all the demand at its bi-weekly auction on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "There has been strong pressure on the available dollars in the interbank market as a result of tight liquidity and consistent depreciation of the naira at the official window," one dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the bulk of the unmet demand at the official window was redirected to the interbank market, putting further pressure on the available dollars floating round Africa's biggest oil producer.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $300 million at 153.21 to the dollar at its auction on Wednesday, short of the $389.86 million demanded and the $499.89 million sold at 153.05 at Monday's auction.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the rate to trend up until there is large inflow of dollars from energy companies towards the end of next week," another dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Most energy firms sell dollars regularly on monthly cycle to selected banks to obtain naira to fund their local obligations.\par \par \uc2 GHANA\par \par \uc2 Pressure may continue on the Ghanaian cedi next week on the back of increasing demand for the dollar by companies.\par \par \uc2 The cedi opened this week at 1.5090 but declined to 1.5160 at the close of Wednesday's trading, mainly due to increasing demand for the greenback from companies, including the oil and gas sector, Biggles Amponsah of Access Bank Ghana said.\par \par \uc2 "The demand from corporate bodies keeps rising, even beyond the expectations of some of us," he said.\par \par \uc2 At midday Thursday, the cedi was offered against the dollar at 1.5165.\par \par \uc2 "Though there is inflow from the mining sector and from mining support service companies, the demand from the Oil & Gas sector and other importing firms outweighs the supply," he added.\par \par \uc2 Amponsah sees the pair trading within the 1.5150 and 1.5190 band next week with the greenback facing a new resistance at 1.52.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Brobbey of \uc2 Barclays Bank Ghana\uc2  said corporate demands for the dollar have pushed the GHS towards 1.52.\par \par \uc2 "Corporate demand is relatively firm amid a general dip in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply and we expect the next key resistance level of $1.5200 to hold next week," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Oludare Mayowa, Barry Malone, Fumbuka Ng'wanakilala and Kevin Mwanza; Editing by John Stonestreet/Toby Chopra)\par \par \uc2 AFRICA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 /   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110519e75j0019e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
