\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-No  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  planned on  \highlight22\uc2 Kenya \highlight\uc2  shilling -cbank\b0\par\par\uc2 340 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2010\par 00:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Import bill rising to $1 billion per month\par \par \uc2 * Growth for 2010 seen in 4.5-5 percent range\par \par \uc2 * Inflation seen remaining stable\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Oct 9 (Reuters) - A rising Kenyan import bill is an important factor behind increased \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying and there is no intention to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to influence the value of the shilling <KES=>, the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Saturday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers have grumbled that fundamentals dictate the shilling should be stronger but that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have capped its gains.\par \par \uc2 "Dollar purchases are to protect reserves and to build up reserves, not just for fun," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Njuguna Ndung'u told Reuters on the sidelines of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank meetings in Washington. "Our import bills have risen significantly to slightly over $1 billion a month, so it means that keeping four months of import cover means being in the neighborhood of $4 billion."\par \par \uc2 He said the timing of purchases was in line with the government's need to meet foreign obligations.\par \par \uc2 Kenya's foreign reserves edged up to $3.49 billion in the week to Friday [ID:nLDE6971JC]. They have risen roughly 8 percent in the past six months.\par \par \uc2 Ndung'u gave no indication as to the direction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key interest rate. The rate was left on hold at 6.0 percent last month, and analysts said an easing cycle could end soon given inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 Ndung'u did not express any concern over inflation, which was steady at 3.21 percent year-on-year in September. He said there was no change to the bank's growth 2010 growth forecasts of 4.5-5 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Growth has been strengthening since towards the end of 2008 and we are going to see that (continue)," he said. "Inflation is going to remain stable." (Reporting by Matthew Tostevin; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 KENYA-SHILLING/ (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101012e6a9001ip\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
