\par\par \b \uc2 E.Africa units seen in mixed trade vs dlr\b0\par\par\uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2009\par 12:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, April 29 (Reuters) - The Kenya \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen in choppy trade in the week to Wednesday, while the Tanzania unit is seen stronger and the Ugandan weak, all on respective \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 KENYA\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <KES=> is seen in choppy trade in the week to Wednesday on anticipated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars purchases.\par \par \uc2 At 0940 GMT, leading commercial banks posted the unit at 78.85/95 per dollar compared to last Wednesday's close of 78.40/50.\par \par \uc2 The unit had strengthened to 78.55/65 early Wednesday but ceded some ground after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Kenya\uc2  announced that it would buy $10 million for monetary policy purposes.\par \par \uc2 "The outlook is mixed. We were expecting the speaker's ruling yesterday would give it a boost," said Friday Mwafuga, head of trading at Cooperative Bank.\par \par \uc2 The speaker of Kenya's parliament on Tuesday appointed himself the leader of the house's business committee, diffusing tension that rose in the coalition government over who should hold the position.\par \par \uc2 Despite the good news, the local unit reversed gains after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action.\par \par \uc2 Mwafuga said the regulator bought dollars either to signal that it did not want a strong shilling or to inject some local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a cash-strapped money market.\par \par \uc2 "The idea that they are in the market creates a bit of confusion," said Peter Mutuku, the head of corporate trading at \uc2 Bank of Africa\uc2 . "Because of that, we see choppy trade between 78.50 and 79.20."\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 The Tanzania shilling <TZS=> is expected to strengthen helped by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and non-governmental organisations (NGOs) selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 1,328/1,338 to the dollar compared with 1,335/1,345 at last Wednesday's close. Dealers see the unit between 1,330-1,335 in coming days.\par \par \uc2 "I still feel that they (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will keep on coming in, and some inflows from NGOs will strengthen the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Hamis Mwakibete, head of trading at Commercial Bank of Africa Tanzania.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Tanzania\uc2  sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the week which boosted the shilling.\par \par \uc2 "In the short term I think we will be seeing a strong shilling as long as we see this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Beven Mushambi, a dealer at Standard Chartered Bank Tanzania.\par \par \uc2 Since last Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded $29.25 million on the Interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 The Uganda shilling <UGX=> is projected to trade weaker against the dollar due to a surge in demand from offshore investors and minimal inflows for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 2,215/2,225 per dollar compared to last Wednesday's rate of 2,180/2,190.\par \par \uc2 The shilling is expected to trade within 2,220-2,240 range in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the local unit hit its lowest level this month to trade at 2,225/2,250 on Tuesday prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell dollars in the market to reduce volatility.\par \par \uc2 "Offshore investors are not rolling over their investment in government securities. We have seen a high number of them buying back and repatriating their dollars, which has put pressure on the shilling," said Catherine Bulinda, treasury head at Centenary bank.\par \par \uc2 Traders said end-month inflows are weak with market activity mainly driven by trade within commercial banks.\par \par \uc2 "We have not seen that much demand for dollars from the corporate clients and importers," another dealer said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its monthly review that the shilling has appreciated by 5 percent in April. (Reporting by George Obulutsa in Arusha, Susan Nabadda in Kampala and Helen Nyambura-Mwaura in Nairobi; Editing by Ron Askew)\par \par \uc2 EASTAFRICA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 /|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|AFN|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090429e54t000tw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
