\par\par \b \uc2 Kenya shilling in slight gain, awaits direction\b0\par\par\uc2 274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2008\par 12:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Oct 2 (Reuters) - Kenya's shilling <KES=> strengthened marginally against the dollar in quiet trade on Thursday as the market waited for clear signs on direction, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 At 0940 GMT, commercial banks posted the unit at 73.00/10 against the dollar compared to Tuesday's close of 73.15/25.\par \par \uc2 The market was closed on Wednesday due to a public holiday.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the unit was likely to stay in a range of 72.70-73.30 against the greenback for the rest of this week.\par \par \uc2 "It is only a marginal gain and it is because of the 'new-month syndrome' whereby people expect some appreciation, assuming the traditional end-month demand (for dollars) is over," said Robert Aloo, a \uc2 Kenya Commercial Bank\uc2  dealer.\par \par \uc2 He said the local unit will strengthen a bit more.\par \par \uc2 "This being the first week of the month, the shilling will gain some ground and then we will go back to the same pattern of it being under pressure," he added.\par \par \uc2 The shilling has lost plenty of ground in recent weeks, due to substantial demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by diverse importers ranging from telecoms to oil firms.\par \par \uc2 Regulator \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice last month, through sale of dollars, to stave off a slump in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "After the holiday there has not been much activity. Next week is when we will identify direction," said Solomon Alubala, the head of trading at Chase Bank. (Reporting by Duncan Miriri; editing by Stephen Nisbet)\par \par 