\par\par \b \uc2 E.Africa units seen in mixed trade in next week\b0\par\par\uc2 602 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2008\par 15:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Sept 24 (Reuters) - The Kenya and Uganda shillings are expected to weaken further against the dollar in the coming week to Wednesday, while the Tanzanian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to strengthen slightly, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 KENYA\par \par \uc2 Kenya's shilling <KES=> is expected to ease further due to increased demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The local unit hit an eight month low versus the greenback last week, as the financial crisis in global markets extended to Kenya, causing volatile trade in the shilling.\par \par \uc2 To stem the shilling's fall, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and auctioned a total of $20 million on two consecutive days. \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rarely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 The shilling weakened to 73.45/55 at 0730 GMT from 73.00/10 at Tuesday's close. It ended last Wednesday's trade at 72.90/73.00.\par \par \uc2 "Holding other factors constant, and without any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities, the shilling should weaken even further due to demand pressure" for dollars, a market report by \uc2 Bank of Africa\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers were bearish longer term if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from selling more dollars.\par \par \uc2 "A weaker shilling could bring about inflationary pressures," \uc2 Bank of Africa\uc2 's report added.\par \par \uc2 But another dealer said the current levels would be attractive to some market players.\par \par \uc2 "This is a good level for exporters, we could see renewed interest...74 could be a ceiling for the very short term," said Noah Meely, a senior treasury manager at Citibank.\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 The Tanzania shilling <TZS=> is seen stronger against the dollar in the coming week, helped by U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows from the agriculture sector, donors and corporate clients selling dollars to pay shilling-denominated taxes.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 1,170/1,175 to the dollar, compared with 1,155/1,160 at last week's close.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said they expect it to trade in the 1,150-1,176 range in the coming week, with a bias for a stronger shilling, with dealers seeing 1,170 as resistance to further weakening for the local unit.\par \par \uc2 "Next week we expect that there will be flows from donors. \uc2 Bank of Tanzania\uc2  will have to sterilize these funds," Christopher Makombe, head of trading at Standard Chartered Tanzania.\par \par \uc2 "We are looking forward for inflows from the agriculture sector, mainly cashew nuts."\par \par \uc2 The shilling is also expected to gain from companies seeking to pay their quarterly taxes.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the shilling eased during the week, undermined by banks covering their short positions, as well as buying by clients who had stayed out hoping the local unit would strengthen.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 The Uganda shilling <UGX=> is projected to edge downwards against the greenback in the coming week, with an anticipated spike in corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks in Kampala posted the local unit at 1,660/1,665 to the dollar, compared with last Wednesday's closing rate of 1,642/1,647.\par \par \uc2 The depreciation trend is expected to continue in the coming days with a trading range of 1,650-1,660.\par \par \uc2 "We are likely to see increased pressure on the local unit as corporate demand increases amid minimal inflows," said Ronald Mutebi, a dealer with \uc2 Bank of Africa Uganda\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the anticipated inflows from offshore traders looking to buy government securities did not materialise.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Helen Nyambura-Mwaura in Nairobi, Susan Nabadda in Kampala and George Obulutsa in Dar es Salaam; Editing by Ron Askew)\par \par \uc2 EASTAFRICA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 /|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|AFN|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080924e49o000zu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
