\par\par \b \uc2 Kenya to revise inflation basket - stats bureau\b0\par\par\uc2 By Helen Nyambura-Mwaura   \par \uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2008\par 13:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Kenya is revising the contents of its consumer prices basket and how it computes inflation, which analysts expect will shave a few percentage points off the country's spiralling rate.\par \par \uc2 The Kenya National Bureau of Statistics (KNBS) said it will change the CPI basket contents that were based on a household budget survey in 1993/94 to a study done over 2005/06.\par \par \uc2 In August, inflation was at 27.6 percent compared with a monthly average 9.8 percent for 2007.\par \par \uc2 The new basket reflects new consumption patterns such as mobile phone charges, bottled water and bicycle taxi fares.\par \par \uc2 KNBS will also change the method for deriving inflation to a geometric mean from an arithmetic one, KNBS officials told Reuters this week.\par \par \uc2 "For ease of comparison, there is a recommendation that worldwide, people harmonise the CPI methodology and most countries are moving to the geometric mean," said James Gatungu, KNBS's principal economist.\par \par \uc2 He said the bureau would start collecting prices in September and would not publish them for 12 months as Kenya reports inflation on a month-on-month basis. The old and new systems will run concurrently for a while, he said.\par \par \uc2 Razia Khan, Africa economist for Standard Chartered Bank, said Kenya was not doing itself any favours by having inflation that was almost twice or thrice that of neighbours already using the geometric mean.\par \par \uc2 Tanzania's inflation in August was 9.8 percent while Uganda's rose to 15.6 percent. Both border Kenya and belong to a regional customs union.\par \par \uc2 FOOD COSTS\par \par \uc2 Khan estimated the new method would probably cut inflation by more than 10 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 "Intuitively, there is some support for the idea that existing inflation is overstated. Once the new CPI index is adopted we may be looking at an inflation rate in the region of 13-15 percent," Khan told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 KNBS is reviewing the weighting for food and non-alcoholic drinks index to 50.5 percent to 40.3 percent which some critics say is intended to mask inflation amongst the poorest.\par \par \uc2 "They are trying to reduce the percentage component of food which I don't think is applicable to a lot of people," said Robert Shaw, an commentator on Kenya's economy. "Our poor will spend a bigger portion of their income on food."\par \par \uc2 Gatungu however said the study a couple of years ago showed Kenyans on average were spending less on food.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is expected to rise on impending food shortages although fuel pump prices are expected to drop, Shaw said.\par \par \uc2 Another analyst said he saw inflation falling to just below 20 percent by the end of the year, assuming decent rains and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  not depreciating any further from current levels.\par \par \uc2 "In January, it could be significantly lower than that, down to 12-13 percent," said Stephen Bailey-Smith, Standard Bank's head of research for Africa.\par \par \uc2 The local unit sunk to eight-month lows against the dollar this week as the meltdown in international markets extended to \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has however \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by auctioning dollars in the interbank market.\par \par \uc2 KENYA-INFLATION/|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AFN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080919e49j000wu\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb